  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1051 PM CDT THU AUG 13 2026\n\nAs of writing, a few showers and storms are lingering across our\nsouthern counties as an outflow boundary slowly works north\nacross the state. Based on persistence from the last few nights,\nadditional development is likely over the next couple of hours\nwith activity generally fizzling out by midnight. These storms\nwould mainly produce brief heavy rainfall and gusty winds.\n\nLatest water vapor imagery reveals upper level ridging\nstrengthening across the southeastern CONUS. At the surface, high\npressure is currently centered across the Northern Gulf. This\nfeature will likely remain anchored across the Gulf through the\nwork week with a slight jaunt to the north over the weekend. The\nupper level ridge will expand and strengthen through the week,\nallowing for temps to steadily warm up through the week with\nhighs eventually settling into the 95-101F range for most of\nCentral Alabama. Continued humid conditions combined with these\ntemps will bump heat indices into the 105-110 degree range. This\nwill lead to a major to extreme heat risk for Central Alabama\nlate this week and over the weekend. A Heat Advisory is currently\nin effect through Saturday evening. These will likely be extended\nthrough the remainder of the weekend.  A few locations may\nbriefly reach 110-112 degrees Friday afternoon. Confidence in\nareal coverage is not high enough to warrant an upgrade to an\nExcessive Heat Warning at this time. We will need to monitor the\ntrends through the day tomorrow.\n\nThis pattern will promote a typically summertime convection\nregime with low to moderate (20-50%) chances for showers and\nstorms. Greatest chances for showers and storms will be across\nour southern areas as deeper moisture spreads inland. Those\nacross central and north-central Alabama will likely remain dry\nas ridging helps keep a lid on things.\n\n95/Castillo",
